  <h2 id="history">A Brief History of AVI</h2>
  <p>AVI, which stands for <strong>Audio Video Interleave</strong>, is a multimedia container format introduced by Microsoft all the way back in 1992 as part of its Video for Windows software. For over a decade, it was the de facto standard for video files on the internet and on CD-ROMs.</p>

  <h2 id="how-it-works">How AVI Works</h2>
  <p>Like MP4, AVI is a <em>container</em>, not a codec. It "interleaves" audio and video data together. Back in the day, AVI files typically contained video compressed with older, less efficient codecs like DivX or Xvid.</p>

  <h2 id="why-obsolete">Why is it Considered Obsolete?</h2>
  <p>By today's standards, AVI is a legacy format with severe limitations:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Inefficient Compression:</strong> AVI was not designed for modern, high-efficiency codecs like H.264 or H.265. While technically possible to hack these codecs into an AVI container, it causes playback issues.</li>
    <li><strong>Massive File Sizes:</strong> Because it relies on older compression technology, an AVI file is often 2x to 5x larger than an MP4 file of the exact same visual quality.</li>
    <li><strong>Poor Web Support:</strong> Native HTML5 video players in modern web browsers (Chrome, Safari, Firefox) do not support AVI playback. You cannot stream an AVI file natively on the web.</li>
    <li><strong>No Advanced Features:</strong> AVI lacks robust support for soft subtitles (like SRT), multiple audio tracks, or chapter markers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="should-you-download">Should You Download AVI Files?</h2>
  <p>If a downloader gives you the choice between MP4 and AVI, <strong>always choose MP4</strong>. The only reason to download an AVI file today is if you are trying to play a video on an ancient piece of hardware (like an early 2000s DVD player with a USB port) that doesn't support modern MP4 containers.</p>
